  "query_preview": "..\nIssued at 1057 PM CST Thu Nov 26 2026\n\nMild temperatures are expected Friday and Saturday across\ncentral and western Kansas as 850mb temperature anomalies sit\naround +7-10C. Northwesterly downslope flow will aid in the\nhigher temperatures as well. Overall, expect a pleasant day,\nespecially Friday. High temperatures are expected to be mostly\nin the 60s and lows Friday night into Saturday morning in the\nmid to upper 30s. The pleasantness to Saturday will depend on\nthe timing of the upcoming system's arrival.\n\nThe main weather impact for the entire forecast period will be a\nprogressive, but potent storm system that is expected to arrive Saturday.\nThe main impact will be from strong northwesterly winds just \nbehind the cold front. Latest short range model trend have been\na quicker arrival of the system and subsequent cold front. This\nearlier scenario would allow mixing to be maximized, and \ntherefore higher wind gusts. Maximum wind gusts of 50-55 mph \nare looking probable given this scenario, with isolated high \nwind criteria (58 mph or greater gusts) being met. The greatest\nchance for criteria to be met is across far western zones closer\nto the Colorado border, therefore a High Wind Watch has been\nissued for the western two rows of counties. This is strongly \nevident on the 00z HRRR/RRFS solutions. Even the later arriving \nsolutions like the ENS/GFS/GDPS still show 45-55 mph gusts. So \nat the minimum, at least some disruptive travel is expected, \nwith the high-end solution being isolated power outages. \nExpected highest POPs are still across the northwest counties, \nnorth of Route 50 up towards the I-70 corridor. Rain showers are\nthe likely p-type, but still do not want to rule out some \nsnowflakes trying to mix in at the very tail end. Given that, \nand the limited residence time in the forecast area, impacts \nfrom precipitation are looking low.\n\nBeyond this system, we cool down a touch into Sunday. Despite\nthis system coming from the north, very cold air is still\nlacking behind it. This will be short lived as temperatures are\nexpected to quickly rise back up above normal into next week.",
